{
  "a1": "During the critical phase, usually on days 3 to 7 of illness, the body temperature drops to 37.5–38°C or less and stays there. At this time, tiny blood vessels may become more leaky, and the haematocrit level increases. This phase usually lasts 24–48 hours. Before the leakage starts, white blood cells go down, followed by a quick drop in platelets. If there is no increase in leakiness, the patient will improve. If there is increased leakiness, the patient may get worse because of lost plasma volume. Fluid may be noticed in the chest (pleural effusion) or belly (ascites), depending on how much leakage and fluid treatment there is. Shock can happen when too much plasma is lost, often after warning signs, and the body temperature can be below normal at that time. If shock lasts a long time, it can lead to organ problems, metabolic acidosis, and problems with blood clotting (disseminated intravascular coagulation).",
  "a2": "Around days 3 to 7, the fever can drop to 37.5–38°C or lower and stay there. Small blood vessels may start to leak, and the haematocrit goes up. This phase usually lasts 1 to 2 days. Before the leaking starts, white blood cells go down, then platelets drop quickly. If the vessels don’t get leakier, the person gets better. If they do, the person can get worse because plasma is lost. Fluid can show up in the chest or belly. If too much plasma is lost, shock can happen, sometimes when the body temperature is below normal. If shock lasts, it can cause organ problems, metabolic acidosis, and serious clotting problems.",
  "repair_notes": "Removed the unsupported claim that 'the blood gets thicker' and kept to the exact information in the provided text while simplifying wording."
}

