{
  "a1": "Continuity of care means your care should not stop when you leave the clinic. Health workers should understand you and your life to give the best care, and keep helping until your problem is solved or follow-up is no longer needed. It is important for long-term illnesses, pregnancy and birth care, mental health, and for helping children grow well. Continuity also means your health information should move with you as you age, when you change homes, or when you see different health workers. Having access to your medical records and discharge summaries, whether electronic, paper, or kept by you, helps choose the right treatment and coordinate care. Missing information can delay care and lead to extra, unnecessary services. For example, in Canada, one in seven people in emergency care had important medical information missing that could harm patients. In the United States, missing information is linked to 15.6% of reported mistakes in outpatient care. Today’s information and communication technologies can greatly improve how medical information is shared.",
  "a2": "Continuity of care means your care should continue after you leave the clinic. Health workers should keep helping you until your problem is fixed or follow-up is not needed. It is important for long-term sickness, pregnancy and birth care, mental health, and helping kids grow well. Your health information should go with you as you get older, move to a new home, or see different health workers. When doctors can see your records and summaries (electronic, paper, or ones you keep), they can choose better treatment and work together. Missing information can slow care and cause extra services. In Canada, 1 out of 7 people in emergency care had important information missing that could cause harm. In the United States, missing information is linked to 15.6% of reported mistakes in care outside the hospital. Today’s technology can help share medical information better.",
